### Executive Summary

**No blocking findings:** There are **no blockers** to publishing [REPORT-2026-11-05.md](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/REPORT-2026-11-05.md) as an initial baseline report. All reported medians, ranges, p99 latencies, RSS values, and allocation counts in the report match the committed summaries in [default/summary.csv](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/results/2026-11-05-m5-max/default/summary.csv) and [one-worker/summary.csv](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/results/2026-11-05-m5-max/one-worker/summary.csv), request/reply IDs and counts are validated, and the narrative appropriately avoids claiming a production speedup or backend winner.

However, source inspection of [baseline.rs](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs), [concurrency/src](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs), and [rt/src](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/rt/src/threads/mod.rs) reveals several **undisclosed benchmark/implementation artifacts** that heavily skew the `threads` burst/allocation numbers, `cpu_paced` tails, and `tokio` comparisons. Fixing two 5-line library artifacts (`Backend::Thread`'s multi-worker `Runtime::new()` and `threads::oneshot`'s `std::sync::mpsc` alias) should precede any Silver-inspired backend prototype.

