### 1. Bugs, Biases, and Misleading Metrics (Ranked by Severity)

#### A. Undisclosed or Partially Disclosed Issues in Code & Report

1. **[High — Implementation & Benchmark Bias] `threads` mode uses `std::sync::mpsc::channel` as its `oneshot` reply channel, plus a second `std::sync::mpsc` collector channel in `burst`/`paced` ([rt/src/threads/oneshot.rs:1-3](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/rt/src/threads/oneshot.rs#L1-L3), [concurrency/src/threads/actor.rs:144-149](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/src/threads/actor.rs#L144-L149), [baseline.rs:322-355](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L322-L355))**
   - `spawned_rt::threads::oneshot` is not a single-allocation oneshot primitive; it re-exports `std::sync::mpsc::channel` ([rt/src/threads/oneshot.rs:3](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/rt/src/threads/oneshot.rs#L3)). Every `actor.request_raw(work)` allocates a multi-producer channel with internal queue state.
   - In `rpc_allocations` ([default/summary.csv:42-46](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/results/2026-11-05-m5-max/default/summary.csv#L42-L46)), `threads` allocates **50.88 MB (`100,637` allocs = 5.032/req, ~2,544 B/req)** across 20,000 requests, compared to **6.24 MB (`60,005` allocs = 3.000/req, ~312 B/req)** for `async`/`blocking`/`dedicated` and **2.88 MB (`20,005` allocs = 1.000/req, ~144 B/req)** for `tokio`.
   - In `burst` ([baseline.rs:322-355](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L322-L355)), 4 producers create 50,000 `std::sync::mpsc` "oneshot" channels without pacing and pipe their `Receiver`s through 4 additional `std::sync::mpsc` collector channels to 4 spawned consumer threads. This explains why `threads` peak RSS jumps to **40.3 MB** and `completion_p99` degrades to **20.74 ms** ([REPORT-2026-11-05.md:93](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/REPORT-2026-11-05.md#L93))—it is primarily measuring `std::sync::mpsc` channel allocation/contention per request rather than thread-actor dispatch.

2. **[Medium — Workload Characterization & Timer Bias] `cpu_paced` (`20k/s`) is actually near single-actor saturation (~95% utilization) and includes macOS `thread::sleep` overshoot in `completion_p99` ([baseline.rs:301-306, 331-340](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L301-L340), [REPORT-2026-11-05.md:109-126](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/REPORT-2026-11-05.md#L109-L126))**
   - In `cpu_paced` and `cpu_overload`, each request runs `20,000` dependent integer multiplications (`process` at [baseline.rs:76-78](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L76-L78)), taking ~45–49 µs of service time (`handler_p99` is 48.3–88.8 µs in [default/summary.csv:27-31](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/results/2026-11-05-m5-max/default/summary.csv#L27-L31)), which caps single-actor service capacity at **~24k–26k req/s** (as seen in `cpu_overload` at [REPORT-2026-11-05.md:117-118](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/REPORT-2026-11-05.md#L117-L118)).
   - Offering `20,000` req/s to a single actor with ~21k–25k req/s capacity puts the actor at $\rho \approx 0.80\text{–}0.95$ utilization. When 4 producer threads pace arrivals with `std::thread::sleep` ([baseline.rs:338-340](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L338-L340)), Darwin timer coalescing causes `enqueue_lag_p99` spikes up to **2.75 ms** ([default/raw.csv:218](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/results/2026-11-05-m5-max/default/raw.csv#L218)) followed by simultaneous micro-bursts into a nearly saturated actor. Because `handler_completion` is computed as `ack.completed.duration_since(scheduled)` ([baseline.rs:301](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L301)), generator sleep lag is baked directly into `completion_p99`.

3. **[Medium — Measurement Window & Driver Overhead] `elapsed`, `Reply::wait`, and allocation counting include non-actor driver activity ([baseline.rs:134-141, 276, 290-292, 322-371](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L134-L141))**
   - `Reply::Tasks(rx).wait(rt)` calls `rt.block_on(tokio::time::timeout(Duration::from_secs(10), rx))` ([baseline.rs:136-141](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L136-L141)), registering and cancelling a Tokio timer entry on the 2-worker driver runtime for *every* request across `async`, `blocking`, `dedicated`, and `tokio`, whereas `Reply::Threads` uses `std::sync::mpsc::Receiver::recv_timeout` ([baseline.rs:142-144](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L142-L144)).
   - `elapsed = epoch.elapsed()` ([baseline.rs:368](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L368)) starts at a fixed wall-clock `epoch` (`Instant::now() + 100ms` at [baseline.rs:276](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L276)) while producers wake from `std::thread::sleep` *after* `epoch` ([baseline.rs:290-292](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L290-L292)) and includes spawning/joining the secondary collector threads in `burst`/`paced` ([baseline.rs:323-355](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L323-L355)). In 13–20 ms `burst` runs ([default/raw.csv:46, 111, 131](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/results/2026-11-05-m5-max/default/raw.csv#L46)), sub-millisecond sleep wake-up and thread-join overhead materially shifts `completed_per_s`.
   - `COUNT.store(config.allocations, ...)` is enabled *before* `gate.wait()` and the 100 ms sleep ([baseline.rs:362-363](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L362-L363)), capturing runtime/driver allocations outside the active request loop (hence `60,005`–`60,007` instead of `60,000` in [default/summary.csv:42-44](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/results/2026-11-05-m5-max/default/summary.csv#L42-L44)).

4. **[Low — Baseline Asymmetry] `Target::Tokio` omits cancellation polling, unwind protection, and type-erased dispatch ([baseline.rs:240-250](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L240-L250) vs [concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs:94-105, 621-647](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs#L621-L647))**
   - `Target::Tokio` runs a monomorphic `while let Some((work, reply)) = rx.recv().await` loop on the 2-worker driver runtime, skipping `future::select(recv, cancel)`, `AssertUnwindSafe(...).catch_unwind().await`, `Box<dyn Envelope<A>>`, and `Pin<Box<dyn Future>>`. In `burst`, `tokio` achieves a 419 µs `completion_p99` vs 3,266 µs for `async` ([REPORT-2026-11-05.md:90-94](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/REPORT-2026-11-05.md#L90-L94)); part of that gap is the per-message `select` + `catch_unwind` + double-box overhead, and part is scheduler locality on the 2-worker runtime.

5. **[Low — Allocator Accounting Bug] `CountingAllocator::realloc` overcounts bytes ([baseline.rs:37-44](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L37-L44))**
   - `realloc` adds the full new `size` (`BYTES.fetch_add(size as u64)`) instead of `size.saturating_sub(layout.size())`, and increments `ALLOCS` even for in-place reallocations.

#### B. Limitations Already Properly Disclosed in Report / README

- **Uncontrolled host & validation overlap:** Unpinned Apple M5 Max runs, OS power/frequency scaling, and the first default campaign overlapping the end of a workspace validation run ([REPORT-2026-11-05.md:9-12, 25-27](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/REPORT-2026-11-05.md#L9-L27), [README.md:24-26](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/README.md#L24-L26)).
- **Statistical aggregation & `/usr/bin/time` scope:** Medians of per-run p99s are not pooled p99s; peak RSS and CPU seconds cover process startup, warmup of all actors, 100 ms pre-epoch delay, sample buffers, sorting, and teardown ([REPORT-2026-11-05.md:23-24](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/REPORT-2026-11-05.md#L23-L24), [README.md:63-65, 77-80](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/README.md#L63-L80)).
- **`queue_p99` & `timing=off` definitions:** `queue_p99` includes producer-side request construction/send and scheduling before handler start; `timing=off` still records completion/observation timestamps, and `CountingAllocator` always loads `COUNT` ([README.md:54-55, 69-75](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/README.md#L54-L75), [REPORT-2026-11-05.md:44-46, 150-152](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/REPORT-2026-11-05.md#L150-L152)).

