### 2. Is the Runtime-Proliferation Inference Justified, and How to Separate Causes?

- **Yes, the runtime-proliferation inference is strongly justified as the primary cause of the `mostly_idle` `dedicated` collapse**, though the follow-up experiment does not yet isolate *which mechanism* inside `Runtime::new()` dominates.
- **Why it is justified:**
  - In [concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs:586-588](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs#L586-L588), `Backend::Thread` spawns a dedicated OS thread via `threads::spawn(move || threads::block_on(inner_future))`.
  - In [rt/src/threads/mod.rs:37-40](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/rt/src/threads/mod.rs#L37-L40), `threads::block_on` calls `Runtime::new().unwrap()`, which constructs a **multi-thread Tokio runtime** (`Builder::new_multi_thread().enable_all()`).
  - On the 18-core M5 Max ([default/metadata.json:10, 273](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/results/2026-11-05-m5-max/default/metadata.json#L10)), 128 `Backend::Thread` actors create **128 owner threads + $128 \times 18 = 2,304$ Tokio worker threads (2,432 actor-related OS threads total)**.
  - In `default/summary.csv`, `dedicated` performs well with 4 actors (`rpc_parallel`: **206.5k replies/s, 13.3 MB RSS, 0.87 CPU-s** at [default/summary.csv:9](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/results/2026-11-05-m5-max/default/summary.csv#L9)), and synchronous `threads` (128 OS threads, 0 Tokio runtimes) performs well with 128 actors (`mostly_idle`: **233.5k replies/s, 13.9 MB RSS, 0.72 CPU-s** at [default/summary.csv:15](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/results/2026-11-05-m5-max/default/summary.csv#L15)). Only `dedicated` at 128 actors collapses (**24.7k replies/s, 81.4 MB RSS, 12.56 CPU-s** at [default/summary.csv:14](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/results/2026-11-05-m5-max/default/summary.csv#L14)). Setting `TOKIO_WORKER_THREADS=1` cuts worker threads from 2,304 to 128 and immediately restores throughput to **180.1k replies/s**, cuts `completion_p99` from **251.7 µs to 29.8 µs**, cuts RSS from **81.4 MB to 20.5 MB**, and cuts CPU time from **12.56 s to 1.01 s** ([one-worker/summary.csv:9](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/results/2026-11-05-m5-max/one-worker/summary.csv#L9)).
- **What remains confounded:**
  1. `TOKIO_WORKER_THREADS=1` still builds a `MultiThread` runtime per actor (1 owner thread blocked in `rt.block_on` + 1 spawned worker thread = **256 threads** for 128 actors, hence 20.5 MB RSS vs 13.9 MB for `threads`).
  2. Whole-process CPU/RSS (`run.py:79-80`) and the short 100 ms gap after warming all 128 actors ([baseline.rs:262, 276](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L262-L276)) conflate **128-runtime startup/warmup/teardown overhead** with **steady-state interference from 124 idle runtimes during the measurement window**.
- **Best experiment to separate causes:**
  - Run a **3-flavor × 2-warmup-delay ablation** on `mostly_idle` (128 actors, 4 active) and `rpc_parallel` (4 actors, 4 active):
    - Compare (a) `Builder::new_multi_thread()` (18 workers/actor), (b) `Builder::new_multi_thread().worker_threads(1)` (1 worker/actor), and (c) `Builder::new_current_thread().enable_all()` (0 extra worker threads/actor—runs directly on the `threads::spawn` OS thread).
    - Record **in-window `getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF)` CPU time and RSS delta** around the measured loop (separately from warmup and `wait_exit_async` teardown), and test both warming all 128 actors vs warming only the 4 active actors (or adding a 1s post-warmup settle delay).

