### 3. Three Highest-Value Next Experiments

#### Experiment 1: `Backend::Thread` Single-Thread Runtime (`new_current_thread`) Ablation & Nested-Task Verification
- **Hypothesis:** Replacing `Runtime::new()` in [rt/src/threads/mod.rs:37-40](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/rt/src/threads/mod.rs#L37-L40) (or in a dedicated actor runner called by [concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs:586-588](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs#L586-L588)) with `tokio::runtime::Builder::new_current_thread().enable_all().build()` removes all extra worker threads per actor (reducing 128 `Backend::Thread` actors from 2,432 threads / 256 threads in 1-worker mode to 128 threads) and eliminates cross-thread hops between the `block_on` thread and worker threads.
- **What to measure:**
  - Thread count, in-window CPU seconds (`getrusage` delta), peak RSS, `completed_per_s`, `completion_p50/p99`, and `queue_p99` across `rpc_single`, `rpc_parallel` (4/4), `mostly_idle` (4/128), and `many_active` (32/32 and 128/128).
  - Functional & latency behavior when a `Backend::Thread` actor uses `send_message_on` ([concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs:689-710](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs#L689-L710)), `ctx.monitor` ([concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs:257-309](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs#L257-L309)), and `tokio::time::sleep`/`timeout`.
- **Falsifiable decision criteria:**
  - **Adopt `new_current_thread().enable_all()`** for `Backend::Thread` if `mostly_idle` (128 actors) peak RSS drops to **≤16 MB** (within ~15% of `threads`'s 13.9 MB), `completion_p99` is **≤35 µs** (matching `rpc_parallel`), and all workspace lifecycle/monitor/timer tests pass.
  - **Reject or gate behind an option** if handlers that spawn child tasks via `rt::spawn` require concurrent execution while a single handler invocation blocks without `.await`-yielding.

#### Experiment 2: `threads::oneshot` & Mailbox Wakeup Fix + Send-Only / Burst Profiling
- **Hypothesis:** `threads` mode's poor `burst` tail (20.7 ms `completion_p99`, 40.3 MB RSS) and high allocation rate (5.032 allocs/req, 2,544 B/req) in [REPORT-2026-11-05.md:93, 137](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/REPORT-2026-11-05.md#L93) are caused by [rt/src/threads/oneshot.rs:3](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/rt/src/threads/oneshot.rs#L3) aliasing `std::sync::mpsc::channel`, [concurrency/src/threads/actor.rs:588](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/src/threads/actor.rs#L588) using `rx.recv_timeout(100ms)`, and [baseline.rs:322-355](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L322-L355) piping replies through a second `std::sync::mpsc` collector channel—not by OS thread scheduling or lack of a preallocated ring buffer.
- **What to measure:**
  - Replace `rt/src/threads/oneshot` with a true lightweight oneshot channel (single `Arc` inner state + `Thread::unpark` or `tokio::sync::oneshot` with `blocking_recv`) and wire `CancellationToken::on_cancel` ([rt/src/threads/mod.rs:108-120](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/rt/src/threads/mod.rs#L108-L120)) so `threads::run_actor` doesn't rely on a 100 ms timeout loop.
  - Add a `send_only` benchmark case (`actor.send(work)` with a single terminal barrier request) and remove the `tokio::time::timeout` per-reply wrapper in `Reply::wait` ([baseline.rs:137](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/benches/baseline.rs#L137)).
  - Measure `allocations`/req, `allocated_bytes`/req, `burst` `completion_p99`, `observed_p99`, and peak RSS across all 5 backends.
- **Falsifiable decision criteria:**
  - If a true oneshot in `rt/src/threads/oneshot.rs` drops `threads` allocations from **5.03/req to ≤3.0/req (<400 B/req)** and cuts `burst` `completion_p99` and RSS by **≥50%**, the `threads` burst anomaly is resolved without changing the mailbox transport.
  - In `send_only` and `burst`, if `async` still trails `tokio` by **≥2× on `completion_p99`**, profile the remaining 2 allocations/req in `tasks` (`Box::new(MessageEnvelope)` at [concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs:174, 426](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs#L174) and `Box::pin` handler future at [concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs:99](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs#L99)) to test whether inline/reusable envelope storage is warranted.

#### Experiment 3: Pinned Equal-CPU-Budget & Ethrex/Ethlambda Workload Benchmark Under Overload
- **Hypothesis:** On a quiet Linux machine with equal core budgets (e.g., 4 pinned cores shared across actors and load generators) and a representative Ethrex/Ethlambda message pipeline (mixed control RPCs, shared `Arc` block/tx payloads, and timers), tail latency under high load is dominated by **unbounded queue buildup and admission policy** ([REPORT-2026-11-05.md:109-122](file:///private/tmp/spawned-scenario-tests/benchmarks/REPORT-2026-11-05.md#L109-L122)) rather than cross-core thread migration or `mpsc` atomic instructions.
- **What to measure:**
  - Offered vs completed rate, `enqueue_lag_p99` (using spin-assisted pacing on a dedicated generator core so `enqueue_lag_p99 < 50 µs`), `queue_p99`, `handler_p99`, `perf stat` context-switches / CPU migrations / LLC-load-misses, idle CPU power/utilization, and RSS growth over 60s of overload with unbounded vs bounded (`try_send` / backpressure) mailboxes.
- **Falsifiable decision criteria:**
  - **Proceed with an opt-in pinned/hybrid-wait backend prototype only if** `perf stat` on the pinned Linux baseline shows OS wakeup/context-switch or cache-migration overhead accounting for **≥20% of p99 latency or CPU cycles** at equal core count.
  - **Prioritize bounded mailbox admission control instead** if p99 latency under realistic bursts is dominated by queueing delay (`queue_p99 > 5 × handler_p99`) while wakeup/dispatch overhead remains `<5–10 µs`.

